00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:08.930
YUFENG GUO: Hi, and welcome.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:11.000
We're here at the
security zone at Next

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.050
with Lorena Crowley from the
Google Cloud Security team.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:15.710
Hi, Lorena.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:16.880
LORENA CROWLEY: Hey, Yufeng.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.379
YUFENG GUO: Lorena,
you're an expert

00:00:18.379 --> 00:00:22.530
on ransomware attacks, which are
a common problem for companies.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:23.877
Can you tell us more about it?

00:00:23.877 --> 00:00:26.210
LORENA CROWLEY: You're right,
Yufeng, ransomware attacks

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:29.660
are a common problem for
companies far and wide.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.850
A recent report
on malware attacks

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:35.120
showed that a company is hit
by a ransomware attack every 40

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.200
seconds.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.170
And we're helping protect
employees and companies

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:40.590
from these attacks.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.770
YUFENG GUO: So if
I'm a security admin,

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.320
I'm trying to protect my
company from ransomware attacks.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:46.610
How should I approach it?

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:48.526
LORENA CROWLEY: The first
thing you need to do

00:00:48.526 --> 00:00:51.050
is understand the way
ransom attackers work.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:54.170
One of the most common
attack vectors for ransomware

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:55.070
is email.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.860
As you can see, the attacker
sends phishing emails

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.020
with malicious malware attacks.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.490
They're trying to
trick employees

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:03.770
into opening up these emails.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:07.570
A second common attack
vector is website attacks.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.770
Attackers embed
malicious malware

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.220
into websites that
look legitimate.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.420
And employees may be
tricked into downloading

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.260
the malicious malware,
thinking it's something else.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.940
YUFENG GUO: Wow, so whenever
employees are opening up

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.800
emails or browsing
the web, they could

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.950
be at risk of accidentally
downloading malware.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:26.930
LORENA CROWLEY:
Unfortunately, yes.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.510
And there's even other
sophisticated ransomware attack

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:34.140
vectors when attackers when try
to create new malware variants.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.260
These new malware
variants are designed

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.710
to sneak by existing detectors
and perimeter defenses

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:41.700
that operating systems have.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.540
So as a security admin trying to
protect against these attacks,

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.480
the first thing you
really need to do

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.490
is understand these vectors.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:52.270
And then you need to
understand the business impacts

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.260
that successful ransomware
attacks can have.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.020
Let's take a look
over here at a company

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.540
that has a lot of
ransomware vulnerabilities.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.250
We're looking at a
company here that has

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.500
a lot of legacy
systems that haven't

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:04.880
been updated in a while.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.460
Because of that, there are
several key vulnerabilities

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.340
that ransomware
attackers could exploit.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:11.960
The first vulnerability
this company

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:15.280
has is a legacy
on-premise email server.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.970
These servers that are the
gatekeepers for letting emails

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.490
into users' inboxes.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:21.920
When ransomware attackers
send malicious emails

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.340
into the server, if the malware
detection in the email server

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.140
hasn't been updated
in a while, it's

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.850
going to be easier
for these emails

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.590
to get through to
users' inboxes.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.890
YUFENG GUO: So what happens
when a user, say, opens

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.132
one of these malware files?

00:02:36.132 --> 00:02:38.090
LORENA CROWLEY: Well,
when an employee opens up

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:41.000
one of these files, their
device is encrypted.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.370
And they receive a message
on their screen demanding

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:47.230
a cryptocurrency payment before
the device is unencrypted.

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:50.510
The employee can't access any
of their apps or data, which

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:52.070
makes them unproductive.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:54.050
And the company may
have to pay ransom

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:56.060
to get the device unencrypted.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:57.710
But that's not it.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.870
Another vulnerability
this company has

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:03.540
is that they're using an
out-of-date legacy browser.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.840
When ransomware attackers
embed malware into websites,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.140
these out-of-date browsers
aren't necessarily

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:10.504
able to detect them.

00:03:10.504 --> 00:03:12.170
YUFENG GUO: So that
means you could just

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:14.900
have more employees getting
tricked into downloading

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.812
these files on websites.

00:03:16.812 --> 00:03:18.020
LORENA CROWLEY: That's right.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.820
The impact of an
out-of-date browser

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.010
could mean more successful
web-based malware

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.510
attacks in your organization.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:26.150
If a company experiences a
lot of these different attacks

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:28.020
across their
different departments,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.110
the costs and losses
in productivity

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.760
really start to add up.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.400
One last vulnerability
this company has

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.350
is that it's running a
traditional operating

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:40.250
system that hasn't been updated
with the latest patches.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.470
That means this OS
may let employees

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:45.450
run an executable
file that is risky.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:49.370
So if an employee accidentally
downloads a malware file,

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:50.870
this operating
system isn't going

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:52.940
to do anything to stop it.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:56.120
Ransomware attackers exploit
both known and unknown

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.625
vulnerabilities in
these operating systems

00:03:58.625 --> 00:04:01.020
to lock users' systems down.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.869
YUFENG GUO: Wow, so this legacy
company isn't doing so well.

00:04:03.869 --> 00:04:04.660
LORENA CROWLEY: No.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:07.040
Right now this
company is struggling.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.890
They're suffering with
lower productivity,

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:11.371
and they even have ransom
payments to deal with.

00:04:11.371 --> 00:04:13.120
YUFENG GUO: Yeah, these
ransomware attacks

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.897
seem to be really able to
slow down the whole business.

00:04:15.897 --> 00:04:17.230
LORENA CROWLEY: They really can.

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:19.329
YUFENG GUO: And so as a
security admin, how can I

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:21.550
help protect employees
and the business

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:23.304
from these sorts of attacks?

00:04:23.304 --> 00:04:25.220
LORENA CROWLEY: Well,
I'm glad you asked that.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.520
Why don't we go take a look
at another company that

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.870
is prepared to take these
ransomware attacks on?

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:33.790
This company is
using Google Cloud

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:36.400
to help protect employees
and their business

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.664
from the impact of
ransomware attacks.

00:04:38.664 --> 00:04:40.330
They have multiple
layers of protection.

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:43.360
The first thing this company
is using to protect employees

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.070
is Gmail.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:47.260
Gmail is a cloud-based
email service

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:50.470
that protects employees by
blocking ransomware emails

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:52.200
before they hit their inbox.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.450
Gmail can automatically
detect even sophisticated

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:58.750
ransomware attacks by
identifying them within emails

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:02.020
and blocking them before
they hit a user's inbox.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:04.982
So ransomware attacks can
be detected and blocked.

00:05:04.982 --> 00:05:07.420
Why don't we take a look
at the second protection

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.006
this company has in place?

00:05:09.006 --> 00:05:11.190
It's Chrome browser.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:14.400
The Safe Browsing technology
in a Chrome browser

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.880
alerts users who may be
trying to visit a risky site.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:23.910
As a security admin, you
can add additional controls.

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.800
For example, if you're
leveraging auto updates,

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.360
employees will have the
most up-to-date protection

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:33.670
available, even against
new malware variants.

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:37.590
And with additional protections
like sandboxing and site

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:41.940
isolation, if a user
does visit a risky site,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:45.470
that malware won't spread
between tabs or processes.

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:46.610
YUFENG GUO: That's great.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:50.060
So if an employee browses
to one of these sites,

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:51.500
they're going to get alerted.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.140
LORENA CROWLEY: Right, and
they're going to be safe.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:56.180
The last protection
we'll look at

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:58.670
is how this company is
using Chrome OS to protect

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:00.240
from ransomware attacks.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:03.800
Chrome OS it is a
cloud-based operating system.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.100
Because it runs in the
cloud, it prevents employees

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:10.360
from running executable files,
unlike traditional operating

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:11.760
systems.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:15.390
So even if an employee downloads
a malicious malware file,

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:18.200
the operating system
blocks it from executing.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.880
Chrome OS offers
additional safeguards,

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.070
things like auto
updates that help keep

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:24.990
employees completely protected.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:26.700
YUFENG GUO: So as
a security admin,

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.070
these tools will
help businesses avoid

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:30.980
all kinds of these attacks.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:32.120
LORENA CROWLEY: Exactly.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:35.050
Between Gmail, Chrome
browser, and Chrome OS,

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:38.010
they're all working together
to help provide employees

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:41.700
and businesses the security
they need to stay productive

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:44.885
and to prevent them from paying
money to ransomware attackers.

00:06:44.885 --> 00:06:46.650
YUFENG GUO: That is so great.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:49.830
Thanks so much, Lorena, for
explaining how Google Cloud is

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:52.710
protecting companies
from ransomware attacks

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:53.980
all over the world.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:56.450
LORENA CROWLEY: Thanks
for having me, Yufeng.

